WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.333
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
a Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.333 --> 00:00:05.250
I help some music masters
with their connection quota.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.170
Will we hit the high notes?

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:08.340
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.340
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.530
Meister was adopting
the latest and greatest

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:14.619
in serverless technology.

00:00:14.619 --> 00:00:16.410
And it recently moved
some of their systems

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:18.960
from Compute Engine
to Cloud Functions.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.690
Sadly, one challenge popped
up as the new function

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:24.060
started to eclipse
their connection quota.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.040
But wait a minute--
connection quota?

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:27.990
See, most likely,
your GCF calls will

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:30.200
be doing a lot of
external RPC calls,

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:33.350
querying APIs, fetching
proxy resources, et cetera,

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:33.930
et cetera.

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:36.310
Each outbound request
is a connection.

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:38.110
And there is a quota
involved with it.

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:40.650
So if each one of your
functions is doing 10 RPC calls,

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.750
and you're running
a million instances,

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:44.580
quota might get in your way.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:47.010
Thankfully, GCF has
a little feature

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:49.395
that allows you to reuse
connections between invocations

00:00:49.395 --> 00:00:50.970
of a Cloud Function.

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:52.800
If you're fetching
from the same host,

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.680
you don't have to pay the extra
overhead of the new connection

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:57.240
since it's already opened.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:00.060
For example, code like this
was being used by Meister

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.460
to fetch an asset from
one of their databases.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:04.560
Although this code
is pretty standard,

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.276
the HTTP request
has no persistence,

00:01:07.276 --> 00:01:09.150
which means that it
performs a new connection

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:11.220
on every function invocation.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.920
We can fix this and maintain
persistent connections

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.060
by using a custom HTTP agent
with the Keep Alive option.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:21.690
After running a small test, the
Cloud Function's API quota page

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:24.840
in the Cloud console
shows a huge improvement

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.390
between the two code
paths-- and also has

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:30.030
the side effect of decreasing
DNS resolves, which,

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:32.520
by the way, is also something
you have to provision quota

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:33.060
for.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:36.510
Now, in my opinion, one of the
strongest things about GCF is

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:39.870
it's out-of-the-box support and
helper libraries to the rest

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:41.010
of Google Cloud.

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:42.690
However, because
those APIs generally

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:45.210
make RPC calls through
socket connections,

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:47.880
we can run into the same
problem with connection quota.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:51.420
For example, when using
the Cloud Pub/Sub API,

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:54.150
their code was using one
connection and two DNS

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:55.980
queries per invocation.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:58.800
However, by moving the Pub/Sub
variable to a global state

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.130
and declaring it cons, we can
remove unnecessary connections

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:04.860
and DNS queries,
resharing the connection.

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:06.480
With these simple
changes, Meister

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.230
was able to get an 8x reduction
in connection and DNS usage.

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:12.480
To learn more about our
changes, check out the article

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.170
linked in the description below.

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:15.840
And don't forget to check
out the rest of the Cloud

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.506
Performance Atlas
content-- because when

00:02:17.506 --> 00:02:19.710
it comes to performance,
every millisecond counts.

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:22.460
[MUSIC PLAYING]

